<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Coordinates from Context: 
<br class="ltx_break">Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tessa Masis <em id="id1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(they/them)</em>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Massachusetts Amherst, MA, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Brendan O’Connor
<em id="id2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(he/him)</em>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{tmasis, brenocon}@cs.umass.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Geocoding is the task of linking a location reference to an actual geographic location and is essential for many downstream analyses of unstructured text. In this paper, we explore the challenging setting of geocoding compositional location references. Building on recent work demonstrating LLMs’ abilities to reason over geospatial data, we evaluate LLMs’ geospatial knowledge versus reasoning skills relevant to our task. Based on these insights, we propose an LLM-based strategy for geocoding compositional location references.
We show that our approach improves performance for the task and that a relatively small fine-tuned LLM can achieve comparable performance with much larger off-the-shelf models.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extracting geospatial information from unstructured text is essential for many downstream analyses such as disaster response <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar and Singh, 2019</a>)</cite>, disease surveillance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2013</a>)</cite>,
and historical event analysis
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tateosian et al. (2017)</a></cite>. Geocoding is the task of linking a location reference to an actual geographic location, usually represented by a set of coordinates or an ID in a geographic database.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While there has been lots of work geocoding locations referred to with explicit names (e.g. "in Gaza City"), there has been little attention given to locations referred to with compositional descriptions involving other toponyms (e.g. "about midway between Winsford and Crewe") which occur frequently in domains that don’t have names for geographic regions of interest, e.g. environmental impact statements, food security outlooks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laparra and Bethard (2020)</a></cite>. In such a setting, existing geocoding tools are not able to link the location being described by the entire phrase, only locations mentioned within the phrase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Bethard (2024)</a></cite>.
In addition, most geocoding tools can only link to locations already in a geographic database, which often do not include compositional locations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08741v1/figs/front.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:457/375;" width="457" height="375" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An illustrative example of our task. Given a compositional location description, we expect the model to predict the location’s bounding box (defined by two pairs of latitude-longitude coordinates). </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the task of geocoding compositional location descriptions (see Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and how LLMs can best be leveraged for it.
Our work makes the following contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide findings on LLMs’ geospatial <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">knowledge</em> versus geospatial <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reasoning</em> abilities through task-specific isolated evaluations of each. We find that off-the-shelf LLMs struggle more with generating accurate geospatial knowledge than with reasoning over it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on these insights, we propose the first end-to-end strategy for geocoding compositional location references, leveraging both the geospatial reasoning capabilities of LLMs and the geospatial knowledge bases of traditional geoparsers. Furthermore, our novel use of bounding boxes<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                  
                  
                  
                A <em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bounding box</em> is a rectangular area defined by two longitudes and two latitudes, and written in the standard format of <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathrm{lon}_{min},lat_{min},lon_{max},lat_{max}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>lon</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathrm{lon}_{min},lat_{min},lon_{max},lat_{max}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lon\in[-180,180]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>180,180</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lon\in[-180,180]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lat\in[-90,90]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lat\in[-90,90]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> to link locations provides a simple and informative way to ground locations that don’t exist in a geographic database.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thoroughly evaluate components of our approach, demonstrating that it outperforms prior work and that a relatively small fine-tuned LLM can achieve comparable performance with much larger off-the-shelf models.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geocoding</span>, also known as toponym resolution, aims to link some reference to a geographic entity to the correct reference in a target database or coordinate system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Bethard (2024)</a></cite>.
The majority of previous work has focused on geocoding location references that have an explicit location name, with difficult settings typically concerning ambiguous location names (e.g. "Paris" may refer to Paris, TX, USA or Paris, FR).
Some researchers have looked at geocoding location references without explicit location names, such as indirect location references (e.g. "the Austrian capital") or nonstandard variants of location names (e.g. “Beantown” refers to Boston, MA, USA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dredze et al. (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeLozier et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulkarni et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masis and O’Connor (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Very little attention has been given to geocoding complex or compositional location references, which do not contain an explicit location name and instead describe the location in relation to other toponyms. Thus far, there is only one dataset for this setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laparra and Bethard (2020)</a></cite>. The authors introduce five baseline methods for the task, although none using pretrained language models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Compositional location references must be linked to a coordinate system, not to a geographical database (e.g. GeoNames<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://www.geonames.org" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.geonames.org</a></span></span></span> or
OpenStreetMap<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.openstreetmap.org</a></span></span></span>), since compositional locations often do not exist in geographic databases. While some prior work has examined geocoding without such databases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeLozier et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulkarni et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al. (2023)</a></cite>, they typically link locations to point coordinates which do not represent large or compositional geographic entities well. In our work, we link locations to a bounding box, which defines an area in terms of four sets of coordinates and allows us to ground locations much more precisely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geoparsing</span> is the broader task of both identifying and linking location references in unstructured text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Hu, 2019</a>)</cite>, essentially combining toponym recognition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2023a)</a></cite> and geocoding.
As with geocoding, no geoparsing work to date has addressed compositional location references and methods primarily link location references to a geographic database or a single set of coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs’ geospatial knowledge and reasoning skills</span> have begun to be evaluated in a growing body of work. (We define <em id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">geospatial</em>, following the ISO’s definition<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://isotc211.geolexica.org/concepts/202/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://isotc211.geolexica.org/concepts/202/</a></span></span></span>, as an ’implicit or explicit reference to a location relative to the Earth’ typically involving coordinates or addresses.)
Regarding geospatial knowledge,
recent work has suggested that LLMs learn and use an internal model of space which encodes locations’ approximate real-world geo-coordinates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gurnee and Tegmark (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Related studies have examined the disparities and differences in LLMs’ geospatial knowledge due to factors such as language used <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Faisal and Anastasopoulos (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> or model size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Godey et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhandari et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
To help explain how LLMs may acquire such knowledge, an examination of the Common Crawl Corpus – often used in the pretraining of LLMs – found that 19% of documents contained geospatial data and that a prominent source was Google Maps URLs, which frequently contain both coordinates and a natural language location name <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilyankou et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
However, to our knowledge no work has yet empirically evaluated LLMs as geospatial knowledge bases for downstream tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding geospatial reasoning, recent work evaluating off-the-shelf LLMs has emphasized their promise but also their limitations for geospatial reasoning tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Researchers have found that LLMs such as GPT-4o and Claude 3 Sonnet have limited success with reasoning over geospatial data, struggling to translate qualitative natural language terms to geospatial contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’Sullivan et al. (2024)</a></cite> and performing poorly at tasks like simple route planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Importantly, these studies show that LLMs have the capability to reason over geospatial information in both natural language and geo-coordinate formats.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Thus far, no work has directly compared LLMs’ geospatial knowledge versus reasoning performances. Furthermore, no work has explored LLMs’ geospatial knowledge of or ability to reason over geo-entities defined by bounding boxes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Using LLMs for geoparsing</span> has recently been explored, with methods primarily using LLMs for the toponym recognition step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrod et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2023b)</a></cite>, to generate training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan and Lee (2024)</a></cite>, or to embed natural language descriptions of point coordinate locations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Thus far, LLMs have not been used for generating geospatial information or for reasoning-heavy settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Disentangling LLMs’ Geospatial Knowledge versus Reasoning</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we briefly evaluate LLMs for their geospatial knowledge generation versus geospatial reasoning abilities (see Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Disentangling LLMs’ Geospatial Knowledge versus Reasoning ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
These two capabilities – that is, generating relevant factual knowledge and reasoning over provided factual knowledge –
are necessary for the task of geocoding compositional locations (see task definition in §<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Task Definition ‣ 4 Geocoding Complex Location References ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, below), so isolated evaluations of each will help us understand how to best leverage LLMs for this task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, for our geospatial knowledge baseline we prompt the LLM to generate a structured geospatial representation (either the center coordinate or the bounding box) of a location <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">identified unambiguously by name</em> (Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Disentangling LLMs’ Geospatial Knowledge versus Reasoning ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, top half).
Previous work only examined LLMs’ knowledge of geo-entities represented as center coordinates – here, we include both center coordinate and bounding box representations, allowing us to directly compare LLMs’ knowledge of each.
We include both the location’s name and country in the prompt, since a name can refer to multiple locations (e.g. "San Jose" is a city in both Costa Rica and the USA) and including country information reduces reasoning needed to resolve such ambiguities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For our geospatial reasoning baseline, we prompt the LLM to generate the bounding box of an <em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unidentified location described in relation to other geo-entities</em> (Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Disentangling LLMs’ Geospatial Knowledge versus Reasoning ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, bottom half). These prompts include a description of the location and the center coordinates of locations mentioned in the description. The described location’s name is not included so that the only way to predict its bounding box is to reason over the given information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08741v1/figs/disentangle.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:457/517;" width="457" height="517" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Illustrative examples of our LLM geospatial knowledge and reasoning baselines; all examples refer to the same location. Knowledge baseline: the LLM is given only the location’s name. Reasoning baseline: the LLM is given only a description of the location and mentioned locations’ coordinates (mentioned location names in red, for emphasis).
Point refers to a center coordinate prediction (written as <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{lat,lon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{lat,lon\}</annotation></semantics></math>); box refers to a bounding box prediction (written as <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{lon_{min},lat_{min},lon_{max},lat_{max}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{lon_{min},lat_{min},lon_{max},lat_{max}\}</annotation></semantics></math>).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data.</span> We use the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GeoCoDe</span> dataset, which contains location descriptions from English Wikipedia articles that are linked to the OpenStreetMap (OSM) geodatabase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laparra and Bethard (2020)</a></cite>.
For the current evaluation, we use <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GeoCoDe</span>’s test set of 1,000 manually curated compositional location descriptions. Created by randomly sampling Wikipedia pages, the dataset includes locations from all continents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representing locations.</span> By leveraging the OSM links in <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GeoCoDe</span>, we retrieve gold geospatial information to represent each described location and location mentioned in a description.
Locations can be represented as center coordinates, bounding boxes, or polygons, each with its own pros and cons.
<em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Center coordinates</em>, which represent a location as a point, are used by the vast majority of geocoding datasets and are the simplest way to ground a location that does not exist in a geographic database. While adequate for point locations or small landmarks, they fall short when representing larger entities like states or countries.
<em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Polygons</em> represent locations with an unlimited sequence of coordinates defining a closed shape, so are much more complex and thus rarely used; at present, there are no practical end-to-end geocoding systems using polygons.
<em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bounding boxes</em> represent a location as a rectangle-shaped area via two latitude-longitude coordinate pairs, efficiently encoding basic size and shape.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we primarily represent locations with bounding boxes.
While not used before for geocoding, we argue that bounding boxes offer a happy medium between center coordinates and polygons – they can encode areal information about the location while still being simple enough that off-the-shelf LLMs can reason over them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> We use five metrics to evaluate predictions. Two are common geocoding metrics: <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coverage</em> is the percentage of examples where the model produces a prediction, and <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distance error</em> is the distance between the predicted and gold center coordinates or bounding box centroids. The other three metrics, introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laparra and Bethard</a></cite>, measure the overlapping area between a predicted and gold bounding box: <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">area precision</em> is the fraction of overlapping area out of the entire predicted area, <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">area recall</em> is the fraction of overlapping area out of the entire gold area, and <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">area F1-score</em> is the harmonic mean of the precision and recall (calculated at test set-level, following prior work).
Since these metrics measure area overlap, they are not used to evaluate center coordinate predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span> We evaluate three off-the-shelf open-source LLMs: Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct, Llama3.3-70B-Instruct, and Llama3.1-405B-Instruct. The models are evaluated in a few-shot manner, with two examples included in the system prompt (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Additional Experimental Details ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for full prompts used).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Disentangling LLMs’ Geospatial Knowledge versus Reasoning ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports the results of our geospatial knowledge versus reasoning baselines (also see Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix B Additional Results ‣ Coordinates from Context: Using LLMs to Ground Complex Location References" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> in Appendix). We observe that all models are decidedly better at the geospatial reasoning baseline than the geospatial knowledge one, with lower average distance errors by more than 140km and consistent increases in area F1-score. The Qwen 72B model appears to have the biggest gap between knowledge and reasoning, with a difference of 300km between distance errors and of .164 between F1-scores.
Additionally, increasing model size appears to improve geospatial knowledge more than geospatial reasoning (e.g. F1-score for Llama 70B versus 405B is .137 versus .195 for bounding box knowledge, but .239 versus .235 for reasoning).
Finally, we note that all models have slightly better performances when predicting a point than a bounding box for the knowledge baseline, with lower distance errors of 10 to 50km.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
